WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.650
A threat hunt should produce an outcome even when no compromise is found.

00:00:03.650 --> 00:00:07.260
Finding nothing in a scoped hunt can still be useful if the hypothesis was clear,

00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:10.480
the data sources were appropriate, and the limitations were documented.

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:14.735
The outcome may be increased confidence, a rejected hypothesis, a better question,

00:00:14.735 --> 00:00:16.630
a telemetry gap, or a detection idea.

00:00:16.630 --> 00:00:19.470
Hunting is valuable because it improves the security program,

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:21.930
not only because it occasionally finds an incident.

00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:24.105
One common output is new detection logic.

00:00:24.105 --> 00:00:27.645
If a hunt identifies a behavior pattern that can be monitored reliably,

00:00:27.645 --> 00:00:31.795
detection engineers can turn the query into a scheduled rule, correlation, or analytic.

00:00:31.795 --> 00:00:36.140
The detection should include clear logic, required data sources, expected false positives,

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:38.110
severity guidance, and response steps.

00:00:38.110 --> 00:00:42.430
A hunt query may be exploratory and broad. A production detection needs repeatability,

00:00:42.430 --> 00:00:44.235
performance, ownership, and tuning.

00:00:44.235 --> 00:00:46.000
Another output is improved logging.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:49.755
A hunt may reveal that endpoint process events lack command-line detail,

00:00:49.755 --> 00:00:54.255
DNS logs are missing client identifiers, cloud logs are retained for too short a period,

00:00:54.255 --> 00:00:56.480
or identity logs are not parsed consistently.

00:00:56.480 --> 00:01:00.160
Those findings matter. Better telemetry may be the most important result of the hunt

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:02.780
because it makes future investigations stronger.

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:04.270
Hunts can also tune alerts.

00:01:04.270 --> 00:01:07.355
If analysts repeatedly see benign administrative activity,

00:01:07.355 --> 00:01:10.555
the hunt may identify safe context for suppression or enrichment.

00:01:10.555 --> 00:01:14.240
Tuning should not simply hide noise. It should preserve the signal while reducing

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:15.700
unnecessary analyst work.

00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:20.110
Sometimes the right answer is not suppression but better asset context, identity labeling,

00:01:20.110 --> 00:01:22.050
or an allow list with ownership and review.

00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:24.135
Response playbooks can improve as well.

00:01:24.135 --> 00:01:28.430
If a hunt shows that a suspicious behavior requires checking five related data sources,

00:01:28.430 --> 00:01:29.595
document that workflow.

00:01:29.595 --> 00:01:32.160
If escalation criteria were unclear, refine them.

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:34.420
If incident responders need specific fields,

00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:37.360
add those fields to the detection output or triage notes.

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:40.965
Hunting and incident response should feed each other. Threat intelligence feedback is

00:01:40.965 --> 00:01:41.760
another outcome.

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:45.165
A hunt may show whether a reported behavior is relevant to the environment,

00:01:45.165 --> 00:01:48.600
which assets would be exposed, or which controls already provide coverage.

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:51.540
It may also produce internal intelligence about normal behavior,

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:54.995
such as common administrative tools, expected service-account patterns,

00:01:54.995 --> 00:01:56.235
and typical cloud actions.

00:01:56.235 --> 00:01:58.525
For beginner hunters, the key idea is simple:

00:01:58.525 --> 00:02:01.205
close the loop. Do not let the hunt end at the last query.

00:02:01.205 --> 00:02:04.670
Document the result, preserve useful evidence, identify gaps,

00:02:04.670 --> 00:02:07.840
recommend detection or logging changes, and share what the team learned.

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:11.620
That is how hunting becomes continuous improvement. Follow-through should have ownership.

00:02:11.620 --> 00:02:14.940
If the hunt recommends a detection, someone should own writing and testing it.

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:16.400
If it recommends better logging,

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:20.010
someone should own the platform change. If it identifies an asset context gap,

00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:21.680
someone should own the data update.

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:24.880
Without ownership, hunt outcomes become notes instead of improvements.
